title: Changing a field's type
tags: [[Data model]]
kind: guide
summary: mf retype converts every value of a field to another type, in one revision you can roll back; what cannot convert falls back to a findable default.

A field name's type is fixed by its first write ([[One value type per field name]]).
To change it, ''retype'' the field — every value of that name, in the whole
repository:

```
mf -n music retype rating float
retyped rating to float: 1204 value(s) converted, 0 fell back to the default
```

Any type can be reached from any other: a mistyped field is never stuck. The
retype is one logged revision, so `mf log undo` takes it back. Reserved names
(`mfr_*`, `mf_*`) cannot be retyped: the system owns their types.

!! How values convert

* ''To `string`'' — always, losslessly, by the value's canonical text: numbers and booleans as text, dates as ISO-8601, `ref`/`refbase` as a 32-hex uuid, `tree_ref` as `<parent-hex>/<name>` (`/<name>` for a root), `externalref` as `<repo-hex>:<metarecord-hex>`.
* ''From `string`'' — by parsing that same form (surrounding spaces ignored). `string` is the hub: to reinterpret a value as another reference type, go through it.
* ''Between scalars'' — `int`, `float`, `bool` and `datetime` convert between each other (a float to an int truncates; a bool is `0`/`1`; a date is its Unix milliseconds; a number is true unless zero), except a `bool` to a `datetime`.
* ''Between two different reference types'', or between a reference and a scalar other than `string` — no conversion.

A value that does not convert takes the target's ''default'', chosen to be easy to
find afterwards: `0`, `0.0`, `false`, `""`, the Unix epoch, or `nothing` for the
reference types (`field IS ABSENT`). The command counts them; `POST /retype` also
lists their metarecords (`fallback_uuids`). A `tree_ref` that would break its forest ([[TreeRef forest]])
— a missing parent, a cycle, a second position — falls back to `nothing` too, so one
bad path never blocks the rest. Two values of one metarecord that the conversion
makes equal become one row ([[No duplicate rows]]).
